WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.018 --> 00:00:05.890
(subdued dramatic orchestral music)

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:09.417
- [Narrator] The world's
oceans are under siege.

00:00:09.417 --> 00:00:11.574
Marine life is dying at alarming rates

00:00:11.574 --> 00:00:14.073
due to accelerating
pollution, overfishing,

00:00:14.073 --> 00:00:15.420
and climate change.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:17.637
- People keep saying,
“We're at a tipping point."

00:00:17.637 --> 00:00:20.436
Some people say, "We're
beyond a tipping point."

00:00:20.436 --> 00:00:23.403
- [Narrator] But six miles
off the shores of Key Largo

00:00:23.403 --> 00:00:25.986
and 60 feet deep sits Aquarius,

00:00:27.068 --> 00:00:30.411
the world's only underwater
research laboratory.

00:00:30.411 --> 00:00:33.336
- There is no other place
where people live underwater

00:00:33.336 --> 00:00:36.832
and work underwater for
extended periods of time.

00:00:36.832 --> 00:00:39.102
- [Narrator] The
diver-scientists who work here

00:00:39.102 --> 00:00:41.138
are called aquanauts,

00:00:41.138 --> 00:00:43.641
and they come from all over
the world to spend weeks

00:00:43.641 --> 00:00:47.660
at Florida International
University's Underwater Lab,

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:51.888
conducting cutting-edge
research on oceanic health.

00:00:51.888 --> 00:00:53.963
This elite group faces
danger in the elements

00:00:53.963 --> 00:00:58.130
for the sake of the planet
in a race against time.

00:01:00.593 --> 00:01:01.942
- The research at Aquarius,

00:01:01.942 --> 00:01:05.458
it's being conducted at a critical time.

00:01:05.458 --> 00:01:07.192
Aquarius, Aquarius Reef Base.

00:01:07.192 --> 00:01:09.003
(garbled radio transmission)

00:01:09.003 --> 00:01:09.836
That's a roger.

00:01:09.836 --> 00:01:10.931
We got the main lock on.

00:01:10.931 --> 00:01:14.658
Aquarius is the last
undersea research laboratory

00:01:14.658 --> 00:01:18.299
dedicated to science and
education in the world today.

00:01:18.299 --> 00:01:21.110
- There is no other underwater habitat

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:23.391
doing what we do how we do it.

00:01:23.391 --> 00:01:25.099
- [Aquanaut] It allows researchers to live

00:01:25.099 --> 00:01:26.473
at their study site.

00:01:26.473 --> 00:01:29.650
They can do incredibly long working dives,

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:34.005
dives which couldn't be
done from the surface.

00:01:34.005 --> 00:01:36.025
(water splashing)

00:01:36.025 --> 00:01:37.521
- When you're diving from the surface,

00:01:37.521 --> 00:01:39.923
the deeper you go the less time you have.

00:01:39.923 --> 00:01:42.006
When you live underwater,

00:01:42.932 --> 00:01:46.593
the habitat actually becomes your surface.

00:01:46.593 --> 00:01:51.002
When you dive from the habitat,
which is about 50 feet,

00:01:51.002 --> 00:01:53.403
and you dive to 90 feet,

00:01:53.403 --> 00:01:56.002
you're not really diving to 90 feet;

00:01:56.002 --> 00:01:59.294
you're really diving to
40 feet from the surface.

00:01:59.294 --> 00:02:01.571
So, now what you've done is you've extended

00:02:01.571 --> 00:02:05.346
that time period that you can
actually spend at 90 feet.

00:02:05.346 --> 00:02:07.179
That's where it really separates

00:02:07.179 --> 00:02:09.851
from the traditional
research or work that's done

00:02:09.851 --> 00:02:12.427
from vessels on the surface of the water.

00:02:12.427 --> 00:02:14.092
That makes a big difference.

00:02:14.092 --> 00:02:17.507
- [Thomas] Marine ecologists
use it as a base of operation.

00:02:17.507 --> 00:02:19.011
Others wanna use it

00:02:19.011 --> 00:02:21.444
for extreme-environment
mission operations.

00:02:21.444 --> 00:02:24.692
We do a number of projects with NASA,

00:02:24.692 --> 00:02:27.981
and they say that Aquarius is very similar

00:02:27.981 --> 00:02:31.231
to what astronauts experience in space.

00:02:35.448 --> 00:02:36.995
- Everybody, when they're five years old,

00:02:36.995 --> 00:02:38.891
wants to be an astronaut
or a marine biologist.

00:02:38.891 --> 00:02:40.293
I never grew out of it.

00:02:40.293 --> 00:02:42.202
Living and doing science underwater

00:02:42.202 --> 00:02:44.763
is the coolest thing I
could possibly imagine.

00:02:44.763 --> 00:02:46.749
- What does it take to become an aquanaut?

00:02:46.749 --> 00:02:48.744
We look at people who have

00:02:48.744 --> 00:02:50.856
considerable experience underwater.

00:02:50.856 --> 00:02:52.520
Diving has to be second nature.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:54.474
It's not uncommon for people

00:02:54.474 --> 00:02:56.423
not to finish aquanaut training.

00:02:56.423 --> 00:03:00.018
They start to understand
that, maybe psychologically,

00:03:00.018 --> 00:03:02.904
"I'm not prepared to be
in a confined environment

00:03:02.904 --> 00:03:05.948
"with five other people for 10 days."

00:03:05.948 --> 00:03:08.146
- That's the typical length of missions,

00:03:08.146 --> 00:03:10.097
but we have done longer ones.

00:03:10.097 --> 00:03:12.945
We've done 16-day; we've done 18-day.

00:03:12.945 --> 00:03:16.603
The longest one to date
have been 31 days long.

00:03:16.603 --> 00:03:20.436
- [Thomas] We've saturated
392 scientists now.

00:03:21.864 --> 00:03:24.349
- Both you guys got your
mask, fins, BC, depth gauge,

00:03:24.349 --> 00:03:25.666
pressure gauge, computer.

00:03:25.666 --> 00:03:26.499
- [Aquanaut] Check.

00:03:26.499 --> 00:03:28.632
- I can honestly tell you
from the bottom of my heart

00:03:28.632 --> 00:03:32.898
that I enjoy waking up in the
morning, and then coming to work.

00:03:32.898 --> 00:03:35.176
Yeah, I enjoy my job, a lot.

00:03:35.176 --> 00:03:38.179
- It's not an easy job,
keeping Aquarius going,

00:03:38.179 --> 00:03:42.729
constantly fighting weather,
constantly fighting corrosion,

00:03:42.729 --> 00:03:46.553
but everyday we wake
up and say: "All right,

00:03:46.553 --> 00:03:48.871
"we've gotta do something today because

00:03:48.871 --> 00:03:53.043
"what's happening with the
oceans isn't stopping."

00:03:53.043 --> 00:03:57.210
(funky electronic synthesizer music)

